A||||8||Jim's87GT||Center Console Cover Repair||||1||
Z||000000||Jim's87GT||06-06-2000||08:35 AM||||The cover to the door by my elbow in the console was peeling off at the edges and the hinges were loose.  I drilled out the hinges and used clamps and super-gel to reatach the grey cover.  It was difficult to get it to adhere.  Now the cover on the center console on the passenger side is lifting a little.  Has anyone used contact cement for a project like this or can you offer better fixes?  Thanks, Jim||||reg||1
Z||000001||87ECO5||06-06-2000||10:04 AM||||I used Super Glue and 2" wide masking tape to repair my peeling vinyl problem.<P>I tore off several pieces of tape and had them nearby, ready to attach once the glue was applied.  I suggest the wider tape because, in some cases, you really have to pull the vinyl back into place, and the wider tape is less likely to tear.<P>Even though the glue is supposed to dry in a few minutes, I waited a few hours before removing the tape - just to be safe.<P>Results were great!  The radio/HVAC console was the worst but it's now perfectly flat on the top (where the vinyl usually pulls from the frame).||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroshop||06-06-2000||10:48 AM||||I use the 3m glue which is similar to super glue but designed specifically for vinyl and upholtery repair. It comes in a small plastic bottle and is not cheap. The cost is around $15.00 You can get it at any automotive paint shop or if you want I have some in stock. I use wooden flat paint sticks to hold the vinyl in place once I apply the glue and then use clamps to hold everything down. I have repaired many consuls that way including my own and several customers cars.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||06-06-2000||11:36 AM||||The best stuff to use is LOCTITE!!<BR>"SUPER BONDER" 409 I use this stuff<BR>for alot of repairs of all kind<BR>the part# is 40945<P>They make an even better one it's<BR>PRISM 454 part# 45440 <P>Try it you'll be very happy you did<BR>it is by far the best adhesive<BR>on the market, they are both a gel<BR>type, fills gaps, non-running, irregular<BR>surfaces, poorly matched parts<BR>and porous materials.<P>DXR_DAD<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000004||Monkeyman||06-06-2000||11:37 AM||||Gorilla Glue!  I've never used it but it's supposed to be great stuff.  (And it has a wonderful name, too <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||06-06-2000||01:09 PM||||DaD - 409? 454? dang man........that's Chevy glue!!! -- and you MM, geez......only you would come up with Gorilla Glue!!! 9bet ya searched a loooong time to find that, didn't ya??)<P>And George -- that 3M glue works great when used with the paintsticks (to keep it flat) and the clamps and yeah, yeah, yeah, I know....I shoulda used the paint sticks on certain parts but it doesn't show anyway!! -- mine all turned out looking good tho -- thanks for the help (there <I>are</I> places on the back side of some of those pieces that I repaired that have pieces of paint sticks and pieces of my fingers still glued to them tho!!!)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-07-2000||09:54 AM||||Actually, Gorilla Glue was discussed on the forum long before I ever saw my 1st bottle.  Supposed to work great although it doesn't matter.  As long as it has to do with simians, it's cool <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phoenix||06-07-2000||12:05 PM||||I suggested Gorilla Glue on an old post.  It's a polyurathane based glue and it "foams/expands" while setting up to fill voids. I used it on all of my interior parts that were lifting on the edges  - worked great!  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||86SE||06-07-2000||03:42 PM||||I used 5 min appoxy on my consol.  Works excellent!!||||reg||10
